A||||15||88FORMULA-T-TOP-GUY||So what are the best performing struts for 88s?||||1||
Z||000000||88FORMULA-T-TOP-GUY||06-02-2001||11:22 PM||||I am ready to replace my stock struts and possibly springs.  Do stiffer springs make a big difference in handling?  Which is the best handling strut for the 88 suspension?<BR>Where is the least expensive source for these items? Any info will be greatly appreciated.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||06-02-2001||11:52 PM||||We replace strut on one of our 88s.  We put the Monroe struts on.  Wow what a difference, before you would actually prepare yourself for a bump you saw on the road.  The ride was vastly improved.  I am not saying this was a better performing shock but just that the car rode and handled vastly better than before.  I cannot compare it to other struts other than worn out original struts.  Bottom line we were pleased.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||c m west||06-03-2001||12:26 AM||||personaly I would go with KYB, they are self adjusting and offer a significant range of dampening<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000003||sjp777||06-03-2001||12:45 AM||||Konis are the best, but the $400 price is a little much.<P>The KYBs go for less than half that and offer excellent ride and handling.<P>I guess it depends on what you're willing to spend.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||06-03-2001||01:44 AM||||I noticed the Fiero Store has front KYB shocks in regular and heavy duty, but the regular KYB's only in the rear.  Does anyone sell the HD rear KYB struts?  Is there such a monster?  How would it work getting the HD shocks up front and regular struts in back?  Has anyone tried this?<P>Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000005||lowCG||06-03-2001||11:45 AM||||Kyb's will ride nice,but for performance you need to step up to the plate and go for some Bilsteins or Koni's,if I weren't going for these,I'd get some heavy duty Monroes.||||reg||1
Z||000006||AkursedX||06-03-2001||08:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>I noticed the Fiero Store has front KYB shocks in regular and heavy duty, but the regular KYB's only in the rear.  Does anyone sell the HD rear KYB struts?  Is there such a monster?  How would it work getting the HD shocks up front and regular struts in back?  Has anyone tried this?<P>Thanks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I have this setup, and to be honest i think the front is too stiff.  I would go with the softer KYB's, unless if i lived out on smotter roads, but I drive Buffalo city streets everyday, and they are loaded with bumps and potholes!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||06-03-2001||08:22 PM||||If I wanted a smooth riding car, I would have gotten a Cadillac.  <P>I have the HD KYB shocks and KYB struts.  Ride isn't too great but it handles very nicely.  Only reason I didn't get KONI's was because of the price.  I can't see them being that big of a difference even though I have never driven an 88 with them so I can't say for sure.  Only thing is now I need lowering springs desperately, it looks horrible with all that wheel gap.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||06-04-2001||01:20 AM||||The Monroe struts are better than the KYB struts.  They match a little better as far as stiffness is concerned with the heavy duty KYB shocks.  Konis are way better than anything, but you'll have to pay big money for them.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierobear||06-04-2001||09:49 PM||||What kind of price can you get on KYB's for an 88, and where?||||reg||1
Z||000010||AkursedX||06-04-2001||09:56 PM||||Well there is always the FieroStore ( <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A>  ) they have KYB's for $90 for the fronts and $120 for the rear.  You might also want to try a place like <A HREF="http://www.shox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com</A>  (I think that's what it is) for some cheaper prices.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eric||06-06-2001||07:28 AM||||I can order KYBs from Advanced Auto (priced almost as well as shox.com without shipping).  I don't know about Autozone.||||reg||1
Z||000012||lowCG||06-06-2001||11:03 PM||||Not really the same commodity because I now have no warranty,but with a little adapting, the struts(Bilstein;$107 each)from an '89 Jetta will work just great,and with a little more adapting the shocks(Bilstein again;$50 each)from a Toyota pickup will work in the front.<BR>I did this after throwing away a set of KYBs which are valved 50/50 all the way around,not enough rebound ...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eric||06-07-2001||11:18 AM||||What kind of adapting are we talking about with the struts here, Low CG?  I know your welding and fabrication skills are above nearly all of us on this board, so would you rate this project as too difficult for the average Fiero mechanic?  <P>Am I correct in saying the 88s share the same struts as previous years?  Bilsteins would be very nice in the rear, but Konis are only $150 each vs. $107, and I'm not sure I could make the Bilsteins work.||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||06-07-2001||07:07 PM||||The struts were not too difficult,but I had an extra set of old struts laying around that had threaded collars on the top,so I cut them off and had them welded to the top of the fiero struts.After this was accomplished,I got the Bilsteins,and ground/filed down the landing(1/2") on the shaft where the mounting hardware sits to allow for more travel.The stock bumpstops were made for this type of strut so they just go back in.<BR>I did this over the course of a few weeks so it wasn't such a big job to do all at once.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||qwikgta||06-07-2001||09:57 PM||||I had started a thread like this a few weeks back, I have a set of KYB fr/rr for my 88 coupe.  I ordered them from The Fiero Store.  They only list one part # for the rear fitting 84-88.  When I compare the strut that I recieved it looks like the one on my 87 coupe, not like the one on my 88.  So the question for you 88 GT/Formula guys is---- is there a diff??  I have a 88 GT rear swaybar I am going to put on, will it still fit with the KYBs.  I have not looked into how to fit the swaybar on yet so it may be a stupid question, but i was just trying to stop a lot of work before I start.  Thanks<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
